    "raw": "Given:\n- Jeff takes 6 cans from Tim.\n- Tim buys another half the amount of soda cans he had left.\n- Tim ends up with 24 cans.\n\nLet's denote the initial number of cans Tim had as \\( x \\).\n\n1. After Jeff takes 6 cans, Tim has \\( x - 6 \\) cans.\n2. Tim then buys another half of what he had left, which is \\( \\frac{1}{2}(x - 6) \\).\n3. Adding this to what he had left, Tim now has \\( (x - 6) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - 6) \\) cans.\n4. This total is given as 24 cans.\n\nSo, we set up the equation:\n\\[ (x - 6) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - 6) = 24 \\]\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\\[ (x - 6) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - 6) = \\frac{2}{2}(x - 6) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - 6) = \\frac{3}{2}(x - 6) \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ \\frac{3}{2}(x - 6) = 24 \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\), multiply both sides by \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\):\n\\[ x - 6 = 24 \\times \\frac{2}{3} \\]\n\\[ x - 6 = 16 \\]\n\nAdd 6 to both sides:\n\\[ x = 22 \\]\n\nSo, Tim initially had 22 cans of soda.\n\nFINAL: 22",
